{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-27t","title":"Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests","description":"## Question\n\nWhere are the official sqlite-vec v0.1.9 release assets for macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 published, what are their SHA-256 digests, and what is the verified load mechanism for each platform?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#3](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/3) pins sqlite-vec v0.1.9 on the PHP 8.5+ baseline. Decision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) requires the SQLite Extension Manifest to record \"approved native SQLite extension release, its supported platform assets, and their digests\" and the SQLite Extension Cache at `storage/app/sqlite-extensions/`. The Extension Support Matrix initially covers macOS arm64 (Herd) and Linux x86_64 (CI).\n\nThis ticket verifies and records: the official download URLs (GitHub release page for `asg017/sqlite-vec` v0.1.9), the asset filenames per platform, the SHA-256 digests, and any platform-specific load caveats. Findings feed the SQLite Extension Manifest.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Extension Connection Gate implementation — T2.2 consumes this.\n- Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics — T2.4.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-20","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-2mv","title":"Re-verify the three upstream sample datasets","description":"## Question\n\nAre the Chinook, Northwind, and Sakila upstream sources still available at the URLs and revisions pinned by decision #4, with matching digests, and has anything material drifted in the upstream repositories since #4 was filed?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#4](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/4) audited and pinned the three upstream datasets with checksum verification and a Pin Manifest per `CONTEXT.md`. Time has passed between planning and implementation; upstreams may have moved.\n\nThis ticket re-verifies: the upstream URLs resolve; the pinned revisions are still fetchable; the artifact digests still match; and no breaking schema change has appeared upstream. Any drift is recorded for T4.1 (Import pipeline) to handle.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline decision — T4.1 consumes this research.\n- Schema transformation design — decision #6 stands.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-27","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-3sm","title":"Decide the UUIDv7 implementation strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the UUIDv7 implementation strategy — composer package, in-repo trait + Eloquent observer, or a framework-level mechanism — for Users, Teams, and all sample-domain entities per decision #6?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6) requires \"stable UUIDv7 domain identities\" for users, teams, and all sample-domain entities, while Laravel infrastructure tables keep framework-standard keys. PHP 8.5 + Laravel 13 may offer native UUIDv7 support in the framework — verify.\n\nCandidate approaches: a `HasUuidv7Keys` trait + observer; a composer package; Laravel's `HasUuids` trait adapted to v7; or a framework-native v7 option if 13.x exposes one.\n\nThis ticket decides the mechanism, where the trait/class lives (`App\\Concerns` vs `App\\Support`), and how it interacts with existing User/Team models that currently use auto-increment IDs.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Source Identity Registry that uses UUIDv7 — T3.2.\n- Migration of existing users/teams (they are empty at chart time).\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-24","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-5fb","title":"Decide the Product Reset semantics","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of a Product Reset — how is the Reset Window marked (middleware flag, env signal, DB column), what is the atomic per-domain transactional scope, what does the Reset Evidence record contain (and exclude), and how does Reset Recovery retry?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Product Reset*, *Reset Window*, *Reset Run*, *Reset Evidence*, *Baseline Invariant*, and *Reset Confirmation*. Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) requires \"atomic per-product resets with stable identities, request-time edit cutoffs, hard publish gates.\" Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s *Reset Isolation Proof* mandates a success-and-fault-injection test that \"restores only the target baseline while preserving Domain Identity.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: the Reset Window mechanism; the per-domain atomic transactional scope (where the Source Identity Registry lives during reset — T3.2); the Reset Evidence schema; the Reset Confirmation flow; and the recovery path on partial failure.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline — T4.1 (Reset is the inverse).\n- Reset Isolation Proof test cases — those are T8.x acceptance gates.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-29","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-5zr","title":"Decide the Implementation-Readiness Dossier format and generation strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Implementation-Readiness Dossier's format (markdown, HTML, generated), its location in the repo, and what is generated at build time (CI artifacts) vs hand-maintained in version control?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) requires \"a version-controlled Implementation-Readiness Dossier [that] maps each approved decision to its acceptance gates, automated checks, operator commands, evidence location, and recovery procedure. Generated evidence remains in CI or release artifacts rather than the repository.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: dossier format; repo location; which sections are hand-written vs templated from issue/ADR metadata; how CI attaches run evidence; and the operator-facing navigation structure.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The ADR set — T8.2 decides which decisions get ADRs.\n- Specific operator commands — those come from each stage's tickets.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-37","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-70l","title":"Decide the documentation set per the software documentation lifecycle","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the documentation set produced by this implementation — which decisions get ADRs (per the software documentation lifecycle rule), is there a README + dev setup guide + operator runbook, and how is documentation kept proportional to the software's evolution?\n\n## Context\n\nThe global `rules/software-documentation-lifecycle.md` rule applies. `CONTEXT.md`'s glossary plus the 12 decisions from map #1 and the ~24 decisions from this map are candidates for ADRs. The Dossier (T8.1) is operator-facing; the README is dev-facing.\n\nThis ticket decides: ADR candidate list; README scope; operator runbook scope; how docs track the 8 Acceptance Stages; and the proportional-application rule for what gets documented at each stage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Dossier format — T8.1.\n- Per-feature docstrings — those live with code.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-38","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-7ie","title":"Decide the Search Projection virtual table design","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #40 — Select the PostgreSQL + pgvector + tsvector stack\n- #41 — Postgres schema design for the three sample products\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the PostgreSQL schema for the Search Projection — `tsvector` column + GIN index design, `vector(N)` column + HNSW index parameters, generated vs maintained `tsvector` (`GENERATED ALWAYS AS` STORED column vs trigger vs application-level), and how does transactional consistency between lexical and vector representations work under Postgres's transactional DDL?\n\n## Context\n\n**Re-scoped by the PostgreSQL pivot** — see [resolution of #21](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21). Original FTS5 + vec0 virtual table design replaced with PostgreSQL-native equivalents.\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) required \"transactional FTS with verified after-commit vectors.\" Under SQLite, FTS5 was transactional but vec0 required after-commit listener + verification. Under Postgres, **both tsvector and vector updates are transactional DDL/DML** — the after-commit workaround is no longer required.\n\n`CONTEXT.md`'s *Search Projection* definition (\"lexical representation is transactionally current; vector representation is derived after commit and independently verified\") **needs revision**: under Postgres, both representations can be transactionally current. The embedding generation may still be asynchronous (queue job, T6.3), but that is a latency decision, not a transactional correctness requirement.\n\nThis ticket decides: `tsvector` column design (`GENERATED ALWAYS AS` + STORED vs trigger-updated vs application-maintained); GIN index parameters; `vector(N)` dimensionality from the Embedding Profile (T6.3); HNSW vs ivfflat + index parameters; whether to retain the after-commit verification step as defensive instrumentation or drop it as unnecessary.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Postgres stack selection (ticket #40).\n- Search Document content (T6.1, #31).\n- Embedding Profile (T6.3, #33).\n- Hybrid Retrieval / RRF (T6.4, #34).\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-32","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-983","title":"Postgres extension management approach","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #40 — Select the PostgreSQL + pgvector + tsvector stack\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nHow are PostgreSQL extensions (`pgvector` and any supplementary extensions) declared, installed, and verified across Herd (macOS arm64) and Linux CI — and does the SQLite Extension Manifest framework from decision #12 dissolve entirely or transform into a thinner Postgres equivalent?\n\n## Context\n\n**Supersedes** [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) (SQLite extension bootstrap + diagnostics). **Material reason**: PostgreSQL loads extensions via `CREATE EXTENSION` against installed shared libraries; there is no per-connection load mechanism, no Native Extension Fault class, and no need for the Extension Connection Gate / Vector Capability Probe / Extension Sync Command / Offline Diagnostics / Health Report framework that #12 specified.\n\nDecision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s Acceptance Stage 2 (\"Native SQLite extension capability on Herd and CI\") transforms into \"Verify pgvector available on Herd + CI\".\n\nThis ticket decides: extension declaration mechanism (migration-time `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS` vs post-install script); CI image choice (`ankane/pgvector` vs `pgvector/pgvector` official vs custom Dockerfile); Herd Pro Postgres capability verification; what (if any) `CONTEXT.md` SQLite-specific terms retire vs transform. Likely answer: most of the #12 framework dissolves; the residual concern is \"is pgvector installed in the server?\" which is `pg_extension` catalogue + `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The SQLite-specific framework (Extension Connection Gate, Native Extension Fault, etc.) — closed in [#21](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21), [#22](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/22).\n- pgvector version pinning + tsvector config (separate ticket \"Select the PostgreSQL + pgvector + tsvector stack\").\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-42","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-9xt","title":"Select the PostgreSQL + pgvector + tsvector stack","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the PostgreSQL-baseline stack for this application — Postgres major version pin, pgvector version pin, tsvector configuration (default language, dictionaries, ranking model), and the index strategy (HNSW vs ivfflat + parameters) for the active Embedding Profile's dimensionality?\n\n## Context\n\n**Supersedes** [#3](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/3) (sqlite-vec stack). **Material reason for re-opening**: T2.1 (#20) and T2.4 (#23) research revealed that Herd's stock PHP PDO build cannot load sqlite-vec. The deciding operator pivoted the DB tier to PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector (already running locally via Homebrew); see [resolution of #21](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21).\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) (search tiers, embedding profile, RRF, transactional FTS) **stands** — only the underlying storage technology changes. pgvector's HNSW index is state-of-the-art; Postgres tsvector + GIN is more capable than FTS5.\n\nLocal environment (verified 2026-07-20):\n- Postgres 18.0 (Homebrew `postgresql@18`) running with active worker processes\n- `pgvector` installed via Homebrew\n- `pdo_pgsql` + `pgsql` PHP extensions loaded in Herd's PHP\n- Herd Pro includes managed PostgreSQL\n\nThis ticket decides: Postgres major pin (18.x), pgvector version pin, tsvector configuration (language(s), dictionaries, weights), HNSW vs ivfflat + index parameters for the Embedding Profile's dimensionality, and any supplementary extensions (e.g., `pg_trgm` for fuzzy, `unaccent` for diacritics).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Re-litigating the pivot itself (material reason recorded in #21's resolution).\n- Schema organization (separate ticket \"Postgres schema design for the three sample products\").\n- Embedding Profile provider/model (decision #8 stands — OpenAI pinned).\n- Extension declaration mechanism (separate ticket \"Postgres extension management approach\").\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-40","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-a9v","title":"Decide the Larastan target level and baseline policy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat Larastan level is the target (level 6 / 8 / max with Larastan extensions), and is a baseline file allowed during the build (shrinking over time) or forbidden (forcing zero-violation commits)?\n\n## Context\n\nLarastan 3.9 is installed; `composer types:check` runs `phpstan analyse --memory-limit=512M`. No `phpstan.neon` exists yet. Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s Acceptance Gate model implies a non-negotiable end state but does not specify the path.\n\nA baseline supports incremental adoption through the build; zero-baseline forces per-commit discipline but may stall early scaffolding. Stage 1 scaffold work generates many model classes; strict typing from day 0 has cost/benefit trade-offs.\n\nThis ticket decides: target level; baseline policy (allowed-and-shrinking vs forbidden); where `phpstan.neon` lives; whether `level: max` is the terminal target even if a lower level gates PRs during the build.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Pint formatting rules (Pint is pre-configured; project overrides are separate).\n- CI integration — that is T8.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-18","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-ald","title":"Define native SQLite extension bootstrap and runtime diagnostics","description":"## Question\n\nHow should pinned, checksum-verified sqlite-vec binaries be acquired for supported development and CI platforms, loaded safely on every Laravel SQLite connection, diagnosed across Herd CLI/HTTP and tests, and rejected when platform, architecture, version, or checksum expectations fail?","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-12","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-atr","title":"Decide Filament 5 panel install order and discovery root pattern","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #19 — T1.4 Verify Filament 5 + Shield + Livewire teams starter co-installation (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nIn what order are the four Filament 5 panels (Admin + Chinook + Northwind + Sakila) installed, and what pattern isolates each panel's resources, pages, and widgets into a distinct namespace and discovery root per decision #5?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) requires \"every panel an exclusive `App\\Filament` root\" with resources scoped by product. Livewire teams starter, Fortify, and Flux 2 are already installed; the Filament 5 install must not collide with Fortify's auth routes or Livewire's existing panel expectations.\n\nFilament 5 supports multiple panels via `php artisan make:filament-panel`; discovery roots are configured per-panel in the panel provider. The Admin panel owns cross-product operations only (no Sample Product CRUD, per decision [#10](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/10)); each product panel owns its domain entities.\n\nThis ticket decides installation order, panel-provider locations, discovery-root conventions, and how Shield (decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)) is scoped per panel.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Specific Filament Resources per entity — that is T5.1.\n- Spatie Permission + Shield integration mechanics — that is T3.3.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-16","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-di9","title":"Decide the test pyramid: layers, coverage target, and arch rules","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the test pyramid for this application — which layers (Unit, Feature, Architecture, Browser/Dusk) are mandatory vs aspirational, what coverage target applies per layer and overall, and what `arch()` boundary rules does Pest enforce?\n\n## Context\n\nPest 4 + pest-plugin-drift + pest-plugin-laravel are installed. Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) mandates the Authorization Acceptance Matrix, Reset Isolation Proof, Golden Search Corpus, and Two-Environment Operational Gate — these define the mandatory feature/architecture test surface. Larastan is installed (T1.3 sets its level).\n\nThe app has multiple bounded contexts (Core Application, three Product Domains, Search, Admin) per decision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) — `arch()` rules should enforce that Product Domains do not reach into each other's models directly, and that Sample Panel resources stay inside their owning namespace.\n\nThis ticket decides: mandatory test layers; per-layer coverage targets; the `arch()` rule set; whether browser/Dusk tests are in-scope for Stage 1 or deferred; and the PR-gate vs release-gate split.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The specific test cases for each feature — those live with the feature tickets.\n- CI pipeline wiring — that is T8.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-17","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-dk6","title":"Decide the Search Document shape and Search Surface rules per entity","description":"## Question\n\nFor each Domain Entity type across the three products, what fields form the Search Document, what is the Search Surface (allowed non-sensitive text/metadata), and what Search Tier (hybrid / lexical-only / excluded) does each entity receive?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Search Document*, *Search Surface*, and *Search Tier*. Decision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) requires \"three search tiers, a pinned OpenAI embedding profile, RRF-ranked product-owned results, and transactional FTS with verified after-commit vectors.\" *Search Surface* explicitly excludes raw Blob Assets, contact data, location data, and relationship-only rows.\n\nThis ticket decides: per-entity Search Document field selection; the Surface allow-list per entity; Tier assignment per entity; and how the document retains product identity for Federated Search.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- FTS5/vec0 virtual table schema — T6.2.\n- The embedding pipeline mechanics — T6.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-31","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-ejr","title":"Verify Filament 5 + Shield + Livewire teams starter co-installation","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the current, verified installation and configuration path for Filament 5 alongside the Livewire teams starter (Fortify 1.37 + Flux 2.13 + Livewire 4.1 + passkeys + 2FA), and does Filament Shield coexist with the starter's existing Team policy and Fortify's auth routes?\n\n## Context\n\nThe repo was bootstrapped from the Laravel Livewire starter kit, which ships with its own auth flow, team models, and policies (`app/Concerns/HasTeams.php`, `app/Policies/TeamPolicy.php`, `app/Http/Middleware/EnsureTeamMembership.php`, Fortify response contracts under `app/Http/Responses/`). Filament 5 ships its own panel auth. Decision [#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2) requires Filament 5 + Livewire SFC co-existence but did not specify the install recipe.\n\nFilament 5 is recent; verify against the official Filament docs and any migration notes for Livewire 4 / Flux 2 / Fortify 1.37 compatibility. Capture conflicts with the teams starter's middleware and Fortify's response contracts.\n\nThis ticket resolves a fact-finding question by reading official Filament 5 docs, Livewire 4 docs, and the teams-starter source.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The decision on panel install order — T1.1 consumes this research.\n- Spatie Permission + Shield deeper integration — T3.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-19","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-f8e","title":"Decide the Portfolio Card architecture","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Portfolio Card architecture — Filament Widget vs custom Livewire SFC — and what is the Product Portfolio Snapshot table/view shape that feeds them?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#10](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/10) makes the Admin Panel \"a product portfolio of product-scoped snapshot/query cards, with no cross-product business comparison or warehouse.\" Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) names \"code-owned cards.\" `CONTEXT.md`: *Portfolio Card* is \"a product-scoped display of one Reporting Read Model\"; *Product Portfolio Snapshot* is \"the derived status of one Sample Product used by the Product Portfolio.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: Widget vs Livewire SFC; Snapshot table location and refresh trigger; how cards are registered (code-owned, not user-defined); and how the System Operator's global defaults differ from a team's Team Dashboard layout.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Team Artefact / Team Dashboard — T7.2.\n- Per-card business logic — decision #10's \"no cross-product comparison\" rules it.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-35","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-h3a","title":"Decide the Filament Resource generation strategy","description":"## Additional blocker (added by PostgreSQL pivot)\n\n- #41 — Postgres schema design for the three sample products\n\n---\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- #26 — T3.3 Decide the Spatie Permission, Shield, Fortify, and Team co-existence boundary\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the Filament 5 Resource generation strategy — hand-rolled per-entity resources vs a generator/template approach — and how are resources scoped to their owning panel per decision #5?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) requires each panel to own resources in a distinct subfolder/namespace. Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) defers presentation and generic-form extensions; native Filament resources/forms/tables/pages are \"the explicit exception\" for sample-panel UI (custom UX is native Livewire SFC, per [#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2)).\n\nThree products × ~10–15 entities each = ~30–45 resources. Hand-rolled is controllable; generator-driven is faster but harder to tailor per entity.\n\nThis ticket decides: per-entity hand-rolled vs generator-driven; how `make:filament-resource` is invoked per-panel; how Resources declare their owning panel; and how Shield scoping (T3.3) binds policies per resource.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The actual entity list per product — decision #6 + T4.0 audit.\n- Specific resource pages (Edit, View, List) — those follow from this decision.\n\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-30","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-h7e","title":"Postgres schema design for the three sample products","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #40 — Select the PostgreSQL + pgvector + tsvector stack\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nHow are the three product domains organized in PostgreSQL — separate `SCHEMAS` per product (`chinook`, `northwind`, `sakila`) vs product-prefixed tables in the `public` schema — and how does this interact with Laravel's schema builder, Eloquent model bindings, and `search_path` configuration?\n\n## Context\n\n**Partially supersedes** [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6) (which specified SQLite-conventional product-prefixed tables). The schema *shape* from #6 (UUIDv7 domain identities, `source_id` preservation, deterministic FK remapping, restricted relationships, typed Blob Assets) is database-agnostic and **stands**; only the PostgreSQL-specific organisation question is re-opened.\n\nDecision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) requires each panel to own resources in a distinct namespace. PostgreSQL `SCHEMAS` offer a stronger isolation mechanism than table prefixes. Laravel supports Postgres schemas via `search_path` configuration and `Schema::connection(...)-\u003ecreate(...)`.\n\nDecision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) (import + reset) operates per-product; schema-per-product aligns naturally with per-product reset semantics (drop + recreate schema is atomic).\n\nThis ticket decides: `SCHEMAS` vs prefix; Laravel/Eloquent integration pattern; `search_path` strategy (per-panel? global?); how the Source Identity Registry + Reset Run tables fit; how Federated Search across product schemas preserves product labels (T6.4).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Re-litigating #6's identity mapping (UUIDv7, `source_id`, FK remap) — DB-agnostic.\n- Postgres stack selection (separate ticket).\n- Search Projection design (#32, re-scoped).\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-41","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-j64","title":"Decide the Embedding Profile implementation","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Embedding Profile implementation — OpenAI client package (or HTTP), API key storage, queue job shape for after-commit embedding, and the verification step that confirms the vector was generated and persisted?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) pins an OpenAI embedding profile (provider, model, dimensions, normalisation policy). `CONTEXT.md` requires that \"a Search Document may only be compared with vectors from its Embedding Profile\" — no fallback providers, no mixed vectors. After-commit derivation is required (T6.2).\n\nThis ticket decides: OpenAI SDK vs vanilla HTTP; API key storage (Infisical vs `.env` vs DB); queue connection (sync vs redis vs database); the after-commit listener mechanism; the verification query; and the failure path when the embedding provider is unavailable.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The pinned model/dimensions — decision #8 stands (verify still-current via T8.2 docs).\n- RRF — T6.4.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-33","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-jo9","title":"Decide the Team Artefact schema and Team Owner policy boundary","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the schema for Team Artefacts (Saved Searches, Team Dashboards), how is ownership attributed to a creator (per `CONTEXT.md`'s Team Artefact definition), and what is the Team Owner's authority boundary over them?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md`: *Team Artefact* is \"a team-visible Saved Search or Dashboard owned by one team and attributed to its creator, even after that creator's membership ends. It does not alter shared sample data or restrict a Sample Product.\" *Team Owner* \"has no authority over shared sample data.\" Decision [#9](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/9) separates \"team-owned artefacts from global capabilities.\"\n\nThe Livewire teams starter ships Team, Membership, TeamOwner concept. This ticket integrates Team Artefact as a new domain concept owned by Team but attributed to User.\n\nThis ticket decides: Team Artefact table shape (Saved Search + Dashboard); attribution + ownership semantics; the Team Owner's policy over them; and how a Saved Search integrates with the Federated Search result page (T6.4).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Team-readable URLs / slug policy — out of scope per map #1.\n- Portfolio Card layout — T7.1.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-36","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-lo6","title":"Decide the git branch, PR, and dependency-pinning strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the git workflow for this implementation effort — trunk-based with short-lived feature branches vs long-lived per-stage branches — and what is the dependency-pinning policy (when do composer/pnpm locks get bumped, who reviews)?\n\n## Context\n\nThe repo currently runs trunk-based (recent merge was PR #14 → main). Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s Two-Environment Operational Gate makes every PR run Linux x86_64 CI; release candidates additionally require Herd evidence. Long-lived branches complicate the per-PR gate.\n\nThis ticket decides: branch strategy; PR granularity (per-ticket vs per-stage); merge method (squash vs merge vs rebase); dependency-bump cadence and assignee; and whether Renovate/Dependabot is enabled.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The CI pipeline itself — T8.3.\n- Specific dependency choices — each stage ticket owns its own.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-39","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-mvf","title":"Decide the Vector Capability Probe design","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #20 — T2.1 Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the exact, stable, non-mutating SQL (or sqlite-vec API call) that proves a connection has the vector capability required by the active Embedding Profile, and how is it invoked from the Extension Connection Gate?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Vector Capability Probe* as \"the stable, non-mutating proof that an SQLite connection has the vector capability required by the active Embedding Profile.\" A file-exists check is explicitly insufficient; the probe must demonstrate capability.\n\nsqlite-vec exposes `vec0` virtual tables and distance functions. Decision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) pins an OpenAI embedding profile; the probe must confirm the connection can build and query `vec0` against vectors of the profile's dimensionality.\n\nThis ticket decides the probe SQL shape, its embedding-profile awareness, and how its result feeds back into the Extension Connection Gate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The vec0 virtual table schema for the actual Search Projection — T6.2.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-22","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-oal","title":"Decide the Product Import pipeline shape","description":"## Additional blocker (added by PostgreSQL pivot)\n\n- #41 — Postgres schema design for the three sample products (re-opens the schema organisation question from #6)\n\n---\n\n## Blocked by\n\n- #25 — T3.2 Decide the Source Identity Registry survival-across-resets mechanism\n- #27 — T4.0 Re-verify the three upstream sample datasets (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of the Product Import pipeline — Artisan command vs queued job, transactional scope (one tx per product vs per-table), composite Source Identity handling, and deterministic FK remap strategy?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Product Import* as \"the single pipeline that materialises and validates a Source Baseline in one Product Domain, for both first-time population and Product Reset.\" Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) requires \"one verified Product Import pipeline and atomic per-product resets with stable identities, request-time edit cutoffs, hard publish gates, and compact evidence.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: invocation entry point; transactional boundaries; how composite Source Identities are preserved (decision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6)); how deterministic FK remap works (stable Domain Identity per Source Identity via the registry from T3.2); validation gates; and the publish/abort contract.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Product Reset semantics — T4.2 (co-equal, decided alongside).\n- Per-product schema details — decision #6 stands.\n\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-28","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-q87","title":"Wayfinder — Samples Implementation","description":"## Destination\n\nImplement all twelve decisions from [map #1](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/1) to production-ready completion — a Laravel 13 + PHP 8.5 application presenting Chinook, Northwind, and Sakila as three sample products within one application, with an Admin Filament 5 panel plus one Filament 5 panel per product, hybrid search via FTS5 + sqlite-vec v0.1.9, Spatie Permission + Shield authorization, reproducible Product Import and independent Product Reset, and the version-controlled Implementation-Readiness Dossier — passing the Two-Environment Operational Gate (Linux x86_64 CI green on every PR; Herd macOS arm64 CLI + HTTP evidence on each release candidate), Larastan at the target level, comprehensive Pest coverage, and proportional documentation per the software documentation lifecycle.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Source of plan:** [map #1](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/1) and its twelve child decisions ([#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2)–[#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)). [Issue #11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) defines the eight Acceptance Stages and six mandatory evidence types — they are the spine of this map.\n- **Domain glossary:** [`CONTEXT.md`](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/blob/main/CONTEXT.md) — especially *Acceptance Gate*, *Acceptance Stage*, *Acceptance Evidence*, *Baseline Evidence Fixture*, *Reset Isolation Proof*, *Authorization Acceptance Matrix*, *Golden Search Corpus*, *Two-Environment Operational Gate*, *Implementation-Readiness Dossier*.\n- **Plan-only mode:** each ticket resolves ONE decision; execution is handed off once the way is clear. No ticket carries implementation into itself.\n- **Skills every session should consult:** `wayfinder`, `laravel-best-practices`, `pest-testing`, `livewire-development`, `fluxui-development`, `fortify-development`, `tailwindcss-development`; plus `domain-modeling` / `grilling` / `prototype` / `research` as the ticket type dictates.\n- **Verify version-sensitive claims** against official Laravel 13, Livewire 4, Filament 5, sqlite-vec v0.1.9, and upstream sample-dataset sources. Issues #2, #3, #4 pinned these — do not re-litigate without a recorded reason.\n- **DATABASE TIER (pivot recorded 2026-07-20):** the application uses **PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector + tsvector** — not SQLite. Resolving #21 revealed Herd's stock PHP PDO build cannot load sqlite-vec (T2.1/T2.4 findings), and the operator pivoted the entire DB tier to PostgreSQL, which is already running locally. This re-opens decisions #3 (sqlite-vec → pgvector), partially #6 (schema dialect), and #12 (SQLite extension bootstrap → Postgres extension management). New tickets: [#40](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/40) (stack), [#41](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/41) (schema), [#42](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/42) (extensions). Closed as out of scope: [#21](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21) (T2.2 Extension Connection Gate), [#22](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/22) (T2.3 Vector Capability Probe). Decisions #2, #4, #5, #7, #8, #9, #10, #13 unchanged.\n- **Each ticket sized to one 100K-token agent session;** claim by assigning to the driver before any work.\n- **Repo state at chart time:** bootstrapped (Laravel 13.17, PHP 8.5, Livewire teams starter with Fortify + Flux 2 + passkeys + 2FA + teams, Pest 4, Larastan 3, Pint, Boost, pnpm/Vite, `tests.yml` workflow, composer scripts for `lint` / `lint:check` / `types:check` / `test` / `ci:check`). **Not yet installed:** Filament 5, Spatie Permission/Shield. **Database tier (pivot):** PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector running locally via Homebrew; `pdo_pgsql` loaded in Herd's PHP. No domain code yet.\n\n## Decisions so far\n\n- [Pivot to PostgreSQL + pgvector](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21) — Resolving [#21 (T2.2 Extension Connection Gate)](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21) revealed (via T2.1 / T2.4 research) that Herd's stock PHP PDO build cannot load sqlite-vec. The operator pivoted the entire DB tier to PostgreSQL 18 + pgvector (already running locally). Dissolves the Extension Connection Gate / Native Extension Fault / SQLite Extension Manifest / Vector Capability Probe concepts. Re-opens decisions #3, #6, #12 via three new tickets: [#40](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/40) (stack), [#41](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/41) (schema), [#42](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/42) (extensions). [#22 (T2.3)](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/22) closed in parallel.\n\n## Not yet specified\n\n- Stage 5/6/7 detailed tickets graduate as Stage 1–4 decisions resolve and reveal the panel, resource, and search shape sharply enough to ticket.\n- Per-product variation (Chinook vs Northwind vs Sakila) in import, schema, and search may surface as product-specific decisions once the Chinook prototype (T4.4/T6.5 equivalents in execution) validates the architecture. Until then, the architecture is charted once for Chinook and applied by precedent to the other two products.\n- Product Reset Recovery procedure detail graduates once T4.2 (Reset semantics) is resolved.\n- Browser / Dusk test scope (if any) graduates from T1.2 (test pyramid).\n- **CONTEXT.md revision**: ~15 SQLite-specific terms retire (Extension Connection Gate, Native Extension Fault, SQLite Extension Manifest, Extension Cache, Offline Extension Diagnostics, Extension Health Report, Extension Sync Command, Extension Fault Response, Diagnostic Correlation, SQLite Extension Verification Matrix, Native Extension Verification Matrix, Vector Capability Probe, Extension Support Matrix, Extension Diagnostics Command). Graduates as #40, #41, #42 resolve. Execution task tracked in bd.\n- Acceptance Stage 2 (\"Native SQLite extension capability on Herd and CI\") in #11 transforms under Postgres into \"Verify pgvector available on Herd + CI\" — the stage itself stays, the gate content shifts.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Re-opening any decision resolved in map #1 (#2–#13) without a recorded material reason (new upstream version, incompatibility discovered, etc.).\n- Production hosting topology beyond the Herd macOS arm64 + Linux x86_64 CI targets named in the Two-Environment Operational Gate.\n- Customer-facing music commerce, or merging the three sample domains into one shared business domain (carried over from map #1).\n- The deferred product-facing Team Artefact experience with human-readable URLs and a slug policy (carried over from map #1).\n- Separate physical database files or per-team clones of the sample datasets (carried over from map #1).\n- A separate data warehousing/OLAP enhancement (carried over from map #1).\n- **The SQLite Extension Connection Gate, Vector Capability Probe, Native Extension Fault, SQLite Extension Manifest, SQLite Extension Cache, Offline Extension Diagnostics, Extension Health Report, Extension Sync Command, Extension Fault Response, Diagnostic Correlation, Native Extension Verification Matrix, Extension Support Matrix, and Extension Diagnostics Command** concepts from decision #12 — all dissolved by the PostgreSQL pivot. Closed: [#21](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/21), [#22](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/22). `CONTEXT.md` retains the terms pending revision (graduates as #40, #41, #42 resolve).\n\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-15","labels":["wayfinder:map"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-s5a","title":"Decide the Hybrid Retrieval (RRF) and Federated Search design","description":"## Question\n\nHow is Reciprocal Rank Fusion implemented across lexical (FTS5) and semantic (vec0) candidate lists, and how does Federated Search across Product Domains preserve product labels and route every result to its owning Sample Panel?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) requires \"RRF-ranked product-owned results.\" `CONTEXT.md`: *Federated Search* \"is an Admin Panel search across Product Domains that preserves product labels and routes every result to its owning Sample Panel.\" RRF does not compare raw FTS and vector scores; it fuses ranks.\n\nThis ticket decides: RRF implementation (pure PHP vs package); per-product candidate list shape; product-label preservation; the deep-link route scheme from search result to owning Sample Panel resource; and how Tier-2 (lexical-only) and Tier-3 (excluded) entities participate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Search Document content — T6.1.\n- Embedding Profile — T6.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-34","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-sld","title":"Decide the Spatie Permission, Shield, Fortify, and Team co-existence boundary","description":"## Question\n\nHow do Spatie Permission and Filament Shield co-exist with Fortify's auth, the Livewire teams starter's Team model and TeamPolicy, and the Global Capability / System Operator / Sample Curator roles defined in decisions #9 and #13?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#9](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/9) defines *Global Capability*, *Sample Curator*, and *System Operator* as universal (not team-bound) authorization assignments. Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) requires \"Spatie Permission with Admin-only Shield, selected supplementary activity, and code-owned cards\" — deferring \"presentation and generic-form extensions.\"\n\nThe Livewire teams starter already ships Team, TeamInvitation, Membership, TeamPolicy, `TeamPermission` enum, and `TeamRole` enum. Spatie Permission brings its own Permission and Role models with cache. Filament Shield generates policies for Filament resources.\n\nThis ticket decides: which auth model owns which boundary (Team vs Global Capability); how Spatie's models replace or augment the starter's enums; how Shield's policy generation is scoped to the Admin panel only (per [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)); and how the existing `EnsureTeamMembership` middleware interacts with the universal Product Entitlement.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Implementation-Readiness Dossier's authorization mapping — T8.1.\n- Per-resource policy details — those follow from T5.1.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-26","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-tfb","title":"Decide the Extension Connection Gate implementation","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #20 — T2.1 Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests (research)\n- #23 — T2.4 Verify Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of the Extension Connection Gate — which ServiceProvider boots it, where in the boot order it runs, what fail-closed behaviour it produces on a Native Extension Fault, and how it integrates with the app's SQLite connection configuration?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Extension Connection Gate* as \"the mandatory admission check for an eligible SQLite connection. A connection passes only when its approved native extension is available and its declared capability is demonstrably present.\" *Native Extension Fault* makes the application database unavailable until corrected. *Extension Fault Response* is \"an intentionally minimal HTTP response… communicating unavailability and a Diagnostic Correlation without disclosing host or binary details.\" Decision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) pins the bootstrap and runtime diagnostics.\n\nThis ticket decides: the ServiceProvider class, its boot priority, the SQLite connection resolver hook, the fault response (HTTP 503 with Diagnostic Correlation), and the dispatch path that prevents DB access on fault.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Vector Capability Probe SQL — T2.3.\n- The Extension Health Report CLI — implementation follows from this decision.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-21","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-thd","title":"Verify Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics","description":"## Question\n\nHow does Laravel Herd (Pro/Dot/Custom) load native SQLite extensions at connection time, what configuration or environment variables are required, and is the `sqlite-vec` extension persistent across Herd's PHP-FPM worker restarts?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) requires the Native Extension Verification Matrix to prove \"Herd CLI/HTTP\" capability. Herd is the macOS arm64 target for the Two-Environment Operational Gate. Herd ships its own SQLite/PHP; the verified load mechanism may differ from a vanilla PHP setup.\n\nVerify against official Herd documentation and any community guidance on native SQLite extension loading under Herd. Capture the precise `php.ini` or per-connection PDO attribute mechanism (`PDO:: SQLITE_ATTR_LOAD_EXTENSION` or sqlite3 extension config).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Linux CI load mechanism (standard PHP PDO; covered by T2.1).\n- The Extension Connection Gate decision — T2.2.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-23","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-ym1","title":"Define the authorization, audit, and configurable-dashboard package boundary","description":"## Question\n\nWhich packages, if any, should provide authorization administration, audit presentation, and configurable dashboard forms while preserving the established global-capability, team-artifact, four-panel, and code-owned-card boundaries?\n\n## Starting evidence\n\n- The user approved Spatie Permission v8 as the authorization substrate and Filament Shield as a conditional role/permission administration and policy-generation layer, with panel-qualified permission names.\n- Spatie Activitylog v5 is the supplementary selected-event audit trail; durable Reset Evidence remains its dedicated domain record.\n- Lara Zeus Activity Timeline is only an optional presentation adapter.\n- Filament Shield Enhanced is deferred until a concrete non-CRUD permission requires it and its early-release risk is reassessed.\n- Guardian and Flex Fields are not selected: they either blur the team/data boundary or conflict with the code-owned, allow-listed portfolio-card catalogue.\n\nThe ticket will make the boundary implementation-ready without installing or scaffolding packages.","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-13","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-zio","title":"Decide the Source Identity Registry survival-across-resets mechanism","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #24 — T3.1 Decide the UUIDv7 implementation strategy\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nHow does the Source Identity Registry durably associate a Source Identity with its Domain Identity across independent Product Resets, given that the Product Reset discards the domain's local rows but must preserve identity continuity?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Source Identity Registry* as \"the durable association between a Source Identity and the matching Domain Identity across independent resets.\" *Product Reset* \"discards that domain's local changes while preserving Domain Identities.\" So the registry must outlive the rows it tracks — either by living outside the reset transaction's scope, by being re-affirmed during import, or by being logically separate from the domain tables.\n\nDecision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6) specifies \"preserve exact source identities, normalise values, restrict relationships and deletion.\" Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) specifies \"stable identities\" through the import/reset cycle.\n\nThis ticket decides: the registry's table location (per-domain vs a shared registry table), whether it lives inside or outside the reset transaction, and how it is (re)populated during Product Import.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline shape — T4.1.\n- The Product Reset transactional strategy — T4.2.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:56:31Z","external_ref":"gh-25","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-06r","title":"Wayfinder — Samples Implementation","description":"## Destination\n\nImplement all twelve decisions from [map #1](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/1) to production-ready completion — a Laravel 13 + PHP 8.5 application presenting Chinook, Northwind, and Sakila as three sample products within one application, with an Admin Filament 5 panel plus one Filament 5 panel per product, hybrid search via FTS5 + sqlite-vec v0.1.9, Spatie Permission + Shield authorization, reproducible Product Import and independent Product Reset, and the version-controlled Implementation-Readiness Dossier — passing the Two-Environment Operational Gate (Linux x86_64 CI green on every PR; Herd macOS arm64 CLI + HTTP evidence on each release candidate), Larastan at the target level, comprehensive Pest coverage, and proportional documentation per the software documentation lifecycle.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Source of plan:** [map #1](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/1) and its twelve child decisions ([#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2)–[#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)). [Issue #11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) defines the eight Acceptance Stages and six mandatory evidence types — they are the spine of this map.\n- **Domain glossary:** [`CONTEXT.md`](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/blob/main/CONTEXT.md) — especially *Acceptance Gate*, *Acceptance Stage*, *Acceptance Evidence*, *Baseline Evidence Fixture*, *Reset Isolation Proof*, *Authorization Acceptance Matrix*, *Golden Search Corpus*, *Two-Environment Operational Gate*, *Implementation-Readiness Dossier*.\n- **Plan-only mode:** each ticket resolves ONE decision; execution is handed off once the way is clear. No ticket carries implementation into itself.\n- **Skills every session should consult:** `wayfinder`, `laravel-best-practices`, `pest-testing`, `livewire-development`, `fluxui-development`, `fortify-development`, `tailwindcss-development`; plus `domain-modeling` / `grilling` / `prototype` / `research` as the ticket type dictates.\n- **Verify version-sensitive claims** against official Laravel 13, Livewire 4, Filament 5, sqlite-vec v0.1.9, and upstream sample-dataset sources. Issues #2, #3, #4 pinned these — do not re-litigate without a recorded reason.\n- **HARD CONSTRAINT (discovered by T2.1 / T2.4 research):** Herd's stock PHP 8.5 build has SQLite extension loading compiled out — `PDO::SQLITE_ATTR_LOAD_EXTENSION` is undefined, `SQLite3::loadExtension()` is gated by `sqlite3.extension_dir` (php.ini only, no per-site override), and SQL `load_extension()` returns \"not authorized.\" Laravel's stock PDO-based sqlite connection therefore cannot load sqlite-vec on Herd. T2.2 (Extension Connection Gate) must choose between (a) a custom SQLite3-based Laravel connector, (b) a side-channel `SQLite3` connection outside Laravel's DBAL, or (c) replacing Herd's PHP binary. Findings: `research/t21-sqlitevec-assets/findings.md` + `research/t24-herd-sqlite-mechanics/findings.md`.\n- **Each ticket sized to one 100K-token agent session;** claim by assigning to the driver before any work.\n- **Repo state at chart time:** bootstrapped (Laravel 13.17, PHP 8.5, Livewire teams starter with Fortify + Flux 2 + passkeys + 2FA + teams, Pest 4, Larastan 3, Pint, Boost, pnpm/Vite, `tests.yml` workflow, composer scripts for `lint` / `lint:check` / `types:check` / `test` / `ci:check`). **Not yet installed:** Filament 5, sqlite-vec v0.1.9, Spatie Permission/Shield. No domain code yet.\n\n## Decisions so far\n\n\u003c!-- empty at charting — populated as child tickets close --\u003e\n\n## Not yet specified\n\n- Stage 5/6/7 detailed tickets graduate as Stage 1–4 decisions resolve and reveal the panel, resource, and search shape sharply enough to ticket.\n- Per-product variation (Chinook vs Northwind vs Sakila) in import, schema, and search may surface as product-specific decisions once the Chinook prototype (T4.4/T6.5 equivalents in execution) validates the architecture. Until then, the architecture is charted once for Chinook and applied by precedent to the other two products.\n- Product Reset Recovery procedure detail graduates once T4.2 (Reset semantics) is resolved.\n- Browser / Dusk test scope (if any) graduates from T1.2 (test pyramid).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Re-opening any decision resolved in map #1 (#2–#13) without a recorded material reason (new upstream version, incompatibility discovered, etc.).\n- Production hosting topology beyond the Herd macOS arm64 + Linux x86_64 CI targets named in the Two-Environment Operational Gate.\n- Customer-facing music commerce, or merging the three sample domains into one shared business domain (carried over from map #1).\n- The deferred product-facing Team Artefact experience with human-readable URLs and a slug policy (carried over from map #1).\n- Separate physical database files or per-team clones of the sample datasets (carried over from map #1).\n- A separate data warehousing/OLAP enhancement (carried over from map #1).\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-15","labels":["wayfinder:map"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-08t","title":"Decide the Spatie Permission, Shield, Fortify, and Team co-existence boundary","description":"## Question\n\nHow do Spatie Permission and Filament Shield co-exist with Fortify's auth, the Livewire teams starter's Team model and TeamPolicy, and the Global Capability / System Operator / Sample Curator roles defined in decisions #9 and #13?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#9](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/9) defines *Global Capability*, *Sample Curator*, and *System Operator* as universal (not team-bound) authorization assignments. Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) requires \"Spatie Permission with Admin-only Shield, selected supplementary activity, and code-owned cards\" — deferring \"presentation and generic-form extensions.\"\n\nThe Livewire teams starter already ships Team, TeamInvitation, Membership, TeamPolicy, `TeamPermission` enum, and `TeamRole` enum. Spatie Permission brings its own Permission and Role models with cache. Filament Shield generates policies for Filament resources.\n\nThis ticket decides: which auth model owns which boundary (Team vs Global Capability); how Spatie's models replace or augment the starter's enums; how Shield's policy generation is scoped to the Admin panel only (per [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)); and how the existing `EnsureTeamMembership` middleware interacts with the universal Product Entitlement.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Implementation-Readiness Dossier's authorization mapping — T8.1.\n- Per-resource policy details — those follow from T5.1.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-26","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-2xy","title":"Define verification, delivery, and operational acceptance","description":"## Question\n\nWhat implementation sequence, Pest coverage, schema/import invariants, extension checks, panel and authorization tests, search-quality evidence, reset safety checks, documentation, and local/CI operational gates must be satisfied before this plan is ready to execute and accept?","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-11","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-34f","title":"Decide the Product Import pipeline shape","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #25 — T3.2 Decide the Source Identity Registry survival-across-resets mechanism\n- #27 — T4.0 Re-verify the three upstream sample datasets (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of the Product Import pipeline — Artisan command vs queued job, transactional scope (one tx per product vs per-table), composite Source Identity handling, and deterministic FK remap strategy?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Product Import* as \"the single pipeline that materialises and validates a Source Baseline in one Product Domain, for both first-time population and Product Reset.\" Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) requires \"one verified Product Import pipeline and atomic per-product resets with stable identities, request-time edit cutoffs, hard publish gates, and compact evidence.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: invocation entry point; transactional boundaries; how composite Source Identities are preserved (decision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6)); how deterministic FK remap works (stable Domain Identity per Source Identity via the registry from T3.2); validation gates; and the publish/abort contract.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Product Reset semantics — T4.2 (co-equal, decided alongside).\n- Per-product schema details — decision #6 stands.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-28","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-361","title":"Decide the documentation set per the software documentation lifecycle","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the documentation set produced by this implementation — which decisions get ADRs (per the software documentation lifecycle rule), is there a README + dev setup guide + operator runbook, and how is documentation kept proportional to the software's evolution?\n\n## Context\n\nThe global `rules/software-documentation-lifecycle.md` rule applies. `CONTEXT.md`'s glossary plus the 12 decisions from map #1 and the ~24 decisions from this map are candidates for ADRs. The Dossier (T8.1) is operator-facing; the README is dev-facing.\n\nThis ticket decides: ADR candidate list; README scope; operator runbook scope; how docs track the 8 Acceptance Stages; and the proportional-application rule for what gets documented at each stage.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Dossier format — T8.1.\n- Per-feature docstrings — those live with code.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-38","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-4vf","title":"Decide the Portfolio Card architecture","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Portfolio Card architecture — Filament Widget vs custom Livewire SFC — and what is the Product Portfolio Snapshot table/view shape that feeds them?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#10](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/10) makes the Admin Panel \"a product portfolio of product-scoped snapshot/query cards, with no cross-product business comparison or warehouse.\" Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) names \"code-owned cards.\" `CONTEXT.md`: *Portfolio Card* is \"a product-scoped display of one Reporting Read Model\"; *Product Portfolio Snapshot* is \"the derived status of one Sample Product used by the Product Portfolio.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: Widget vs Livewire SFC; Snapshot table location and refresh trigger; how cards are registered (code-owned, not user-defined); and how the System Operator's global defaults differ from a team's Team Dashboard layout.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Team Artefact / Team Dashboard — T7.2.\n- Per-card business logic — decision #10's \"no cross-product comparison\" rules it.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-35","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-50w","title":"Define team authorization and product entitlement semantics","description":"## Question\n\nWhich users and team roles may enter the overall and product panels, view or mutate globally shared sample data, run destructive resets, and own saved searches or dashboards; and are product entitlements universal or configurable per team?","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-9","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-6je","title":"Decide Filament 5 panel install order and discovery root pattern","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #19 — T1.4 Verify Filament 5 + Shield + Livewire teams starter co-installation (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nIn what order are the four Filament 5 panels (Admin + Chinook + Northwind + Sakila) installed, and what pattern isolates each panel's resources, pages, and widgets into a distinct namespace and discovery root per decision #5?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) requires \"every panel an exclusive `App\\Filament` root\" with resources scoped by product. Livewire teams starter, Fortify, and Flux 2 are already installed; the Filament 5 install must not collide with Fortify's auth routes or Livewire's existing panel expectations.\n\nFilament 5 supports multiple panels via `php artisan make:filament-panel`; discovery roots are configured per-panel in the panel provider. The Admin panel owns cross-product operations only (no Sample Product CRUD, per decision [#10](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/10)); each product panel owns its domain entities.\n\nThis ticket decides installation order, panel-provider locations, discovery-root conventions, and how Shield (decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13)) is scoped per panel.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Specific Filament Resources per entity — that is T5.1.\n- Spatie Permission + Shield integration mechanics — that is T3.3.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-16","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-6ks","title":"Decide the Filament Resource generation strategy","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #26 — T3.3 Decide the Spatie Permission, Shield, Fortify, and Team co-existence boundary\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the Filament 5 Resource generation strategy — hand-rolled per-entity resources vs a generator/template approach — and how are resources scoped to their owning panel per decision #5?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) requires each panel to own resources in a distinct subfolder/namespace. Decision [#13](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/13) defers presentation and generic-form extensions; native Filament resources/forms/tables/pages are \"the explicit exception\" for sample-panel UI (custom UX is native Livewire SFC, per [#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2)).\n\nThree products × ~10–15 entities each = ~30–45 resources. Hand-rolled is controllable; generator-driven is faster but harder to tailor per entity.\n\nThis ticket decides: per-entity hand-rolled vs generator-driven; how `make:filament-resource` is invoked per-panel; how Resources declare their owning panel; and how Shield scoping (T3.3) binds policies per resource.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The actual entity list per product — decision #6 + T4.0 audit.\n- Specific resource pages (Edit, View, List) — those follow from this decision.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-30","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-6qv","title":"Decide the test pyramid: layers, coverage target, and arch rules","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the test pyramid for this application — which layers (Unit, Feature, Architecture, Browser/Dusk) are mandatory vs aspirational, what coverage target applies per layer and overall, and what `arch()` boundary rules does Pest enforce?\n\n## Context\n\nPest 4 + pest-plugin-drift + pest-plugin-laravel are installed. Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) mandates the Authorization Acceptance Matrix, Reset Isolation Proof, Golden Search Corpus, and Two-Environment Operational Gate — these define the mandatory feature/architecture test surface. Larastan is installed (T1.3 sets its level).\n\nThe app has multiple bounded contexts (Core Application, three Product Domains, Search, Admin) per decision [#5](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/5) — `arch()` rules should enforce that Product Domains do not reach into each other's models directly, and that Sample Panel resources stay inside their owning namespace.\n\nThis ticket decides: mandatory test layers; per-layer coverage targets; the `arch()` rule set; whether browser/Dusk tests are in-scope for Stage 1 or deferred; and the PR-gate vs release-gate split.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The specific test cases for each feature — those live with the feature tickets.\n- CI pipeline wiring — that is T8.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-17","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-78q","title":"Decide the Embedding Profile implementation","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Embedding Profile implementation — OpenAI client package (or HTTP), API key storage, queue job shape for after-commit embedding, and the verification step that confirms the vector was generated and persisted?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) pins an OpenAI embedding profile (provider, model, dimensions, normalisation policy). `CONTEXT.md` requires that \"a Search Document may only be compared with vectors from its Embedding Profile\" — no fallback providers, no mixed vectors. After-commit derivation is required (T6.2).\n\nThis ticket decides: OpenAI SDK vs vanilla HTTP; API key storage (Infisical vs `.env` vs DB); queue connection (sync vs redis vs database); the after-commit listener mechanism; the verification query; and the failure path when the embedding provider is unavailable.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The pinned model/dimensions — decision #8 stands (verify still-current via T8.2 docs).\n- RRF — T6.4.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-33","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-7gv","title":"Decide the Vector Capability Probe design","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #20 — T2.1 Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the exact, stable, non-mutating SQL (or sqlite-vec API call) that proves a connection has the vector capability required by the active Embedding Profile, and how is it invoked from the Extension Connection Gate?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Vector Capability Probe* as \"the stable, non-mutating proof that an SQLite connection has the vector capability required by the active Embedding Profile.\" A file-exists check is explicitly insufficient; the probe must demonstrate capability.\n\nsqlite-vec exposes `vec0` virtual tables and distance functions. Decision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) pins an OpenAI embedding profile; the probe must confirm the connection can build and query `vec0` against vectors of the profile's dimensionality.\n\nThis ticket decides the probe SQL shape, its embedding-profile awareness, and how its result feeds back into the Extension Connection Gate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The vec0 virtual table schema for the actual Search Projection — T6.2.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-22","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-8d1","title":"Decide the Larastan target level and baseline policy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat Larastan level is the target (level 6 / 8 / max with Larastan extensions), and is a baseline file allowed during the build (shrinking over time) or forbidden (forcing zero-violation commits)?\n\n## Context\n\nLarastan 3.9 is installed; `composer types:check` runs `phpstan analyse --memory-limit=512M`. No `phpstan.neon` exists yet. Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s Acceptance Gate model implies a non-negotiable end state but does not specify the path.\n\nA baseline supports incremental adoption through the build; zero-baseline forces per-commit discipline but may stall early scaffolding. Stage 1 scaffold work generates many model classes; strict typing from day 0 has cost/benefit trade-offs.\n\nThis ticket decides: target level; baseline policy (allowed-and-shrinking vs forbidden); where `phpstan.neon` lives; whether `level: max` is the terminal target even if a lower level gates PRs during the build.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Pint formatting rules (Pint is pre-configured; project overrides are separate).\n- CI integration — that is T8.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-18","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-adh","title":"Define native SQLite extension bootstrap and runtime diagnostics","description":"## Question\n\nHow should pinned, checksum-verified sqlite-vec binaries be acquired for supported development and CI platforms, loaded safely on every Laravel SQLite connection, diagnosed across Herd CLI/HTTP and tests, and rejected when platform, architecture, version, or checksum expectations fail?","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-12","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-am3","title":"Decide the UUIDv7 implementation strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the UUIDv7 implementation strategy — composer package, in-repo trait + Eloquent observer, or a framework-level mechanism — for Users, Teams, and all sample-domain entities per decision #6?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6) requires \"stable UUIDv7 domain identities\" for users, teams, and all sample-domain entities, while Laravel infrastructure tables keep framework-standard keys. PHP 8.5 + Laravel 13 may offer native UUIDv7 support in the framework — verify.\n\nCandidate approaches: a `HasUuidv7Keys` trait + observer; a composer package; Laravel's `HasUuids` trait adapted to v7; or a framework-native v7 option if 13.x exposes one.\n\nThis ticket decides the mechanism, where the trait/class lives (`App\\Concerns` vs `App\\Support`), and how it interacts with existing User/Team models that currently use auto-increment IDs.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Source Identity Registry that uses UUIDv7 — T3.2.\n- Migration of existing users/teams (they are empty at chart time).\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-24","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-asz","title":"Decide the Hybrid Retrieval (RRF) and Federated Search design","description":"## Question\n\nHow is Reciprocal Rank Fusion implemented across lexical (FTS5) and semantic (vec0) candidate lists, and how does Federated Search across Product Domains preserve product labels and route every result to its owning Sample Panel?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) requires \"RRF-ranked product-owned results.\" `CONTEXT.md`: *Federated Search* \"is an Admin Panel search across Product Domains that preserves product labels and routes every result to its owning Sample Panel.\" RRF does not compare raw FTS and vector scores; it fuses ranks.\n\nThis ticket decides: RRF implementation (pure PHP vs package); per-product candidate list shape; product-label preservation; the deep-link route scheme from search result to owning Sample Panel resource; and how Tier-2 (lexical-only) and Tier-3 (excluded) entities participate.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Search Document content — T6.1.\n- Embedding Profile — T6.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-34","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-bhf","title":"Decide the Search Projection virtual table design","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the FTS5 + vec0 virtual table schema for the Search Projection — column shapes, tokenizers, vec0 dimensionality matching the Embedding Profile, and how is the projection kept transactional for FTS while vectors derive after-commit?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Search Projection*: \"lexical representation is transactionally current; its vector representation is derived after commit and independently verified.\" Decision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) pins the OpenAI embedding profile (dimensionality confirmed by T6.3).\n\nThis ticket decides: FTS5 virtual table schema (FTS5 vs external-content mode); tokenizer choice (porter vs unicode61 vs custom); vec0 virtual table schema with the profile's dimensionality; how Search Document writes propagate to FTS synchronously and to vec0 via after-commit listener; and the verification step that confirms the vector landed.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The embedding API client — T6.3.\n- Hybrid Retrieval / RRF — T6.4.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-32","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-c9m","title":"Decide the Implementation-Readiness Dossier format and generation strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the Implementation-Readiness Dossier's format (markdown, HTML, generated), its location in the repo, and what is generated at build time (CI artifacts) vs hand-maintained in version control?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11) requires \"a version-controlled Implementation-Readiness Dossier [that] maps each approved decision to its acceptance gates, automated checks, operator commands, evidence location, and recovery procedure. Generated evidence remains in CI or release artifacts rather than the repository.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: dossier format; repo location; which sections are hand-written vs templated from issue/ADR metadata; how CI attaches run evidence; and the operator-facing navigation structure.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The ADR set — T8.2 decides which decisions get ADRs.\n- Specific operator commands — those come from each stage's tickets.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-37","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-d39","title":"Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests","description":"## Question\n\nWhere are the official sqlite-vec v0.1.9 release assets for macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 published, what are their SHA-256 digests, and what is the verified load mechanism for each platform?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#3](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/3) pins sqlite-vec v0.1.9 on the PHP 8.5+ baseline. Decision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) requires the SQLite Extension Manifest to record \"approved native SQLite extension release, its supported platform assets, and their digests\" and the SQLite Extension Cache at `storage/app/sqlite-extensions/`. The Extension Support Matrix initially covers macOS arm64 (Herd) and Linux x86_64 (CI).\n\nThis ticket verifies and records: the official download URLs (GitHub release page for `asg017/sqlite-vec` v0.1.9), the asset filenames per platform, the SHA-256 digests, and any platform-specific load caveats. Findings feed the SQLite Extension Manifest.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Extension Connection Gate implementation — T2.2 consumes this.\n- Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics — T2.4.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-20","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-d7u","title":"Decide the Extension Connection Gate implementation","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #20 — T2.1 Verify sqlite-vec v0.1.9 asset availability and digests (research)\n- #23 — T2.4 Verify Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics (research)\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of the Extension Connection Gate — which ServiceProvider boots it, where in the boot order it runs, what fail-closed behaviour it produces on a Native Extension Fault, and how it integrates with the app's SQLite connection configuration?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Extension Connection Gate* as \"the mandatory admission check for an eligible SQLite connection. A connection passes only when its approved native extension is available and its declared capability is demonstrably present.\" *Native Extension Fault* makes the application database unavailable until corrected. *Extension Fault Response* is \"an intentionally minimal HTTP response… communicating unavailability and a Diagnostic Correlation without disclosing host or binary details.\" Decision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) pins the bootstrap and runtime diagnostics.\n\nThis ticket decides: the ServiceProvider class, its boot priority, the SQLite connection resolver hook, the fault response (HTTP 503 with Diagnostic Correlation), and the dispatch path that prevents DB access on fault.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Vector Capability Probe SQL — T2.3.\n- The Extension Health Report CLI — implementation follows from this decision.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-21","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-d8g","title":"Re-verify the three upstream sample datasets","description":"## Question\n\nAre the Chinook, Northwind, and Sakila upstream sources still available at the URLs and revisions pinned by decision #4, with matching digests, and has anything material drifted in the upstream repositories since #4 was filed?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#4](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/4) audited and pinned the three upstream datasets with checksum verification and a Pin Manifest per `CONTEXT.md`. Time has passed between planning and implementation; upstreams may have moved.\n\nThis ticket re-verifies: the upstream URLs resolve; the pinned revisions are still fetchable; the artifact digests still match; and no breaking schema change has appeared upstream. Any drift is recorded for T4.1 (Import pipeline) to handle.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline decision — T4.1 consumes this research.\n- Schema transformation design — decision #6 stands.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-27","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-dsi","title":"Decide the Product Reset semantics","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the implementation shape of a Product Reset — how is the Reset Window marked (middleware flag, env signal, DB column), what is the atomic per-domain transactional scope, what does the Reset Evidence record contain (and exclude), and how does Reset Recovery retry?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Product Reset*, *Reset Window*, *Reset Run*, *Reset Evidence*, *Baseline Invariant*, and *Reset Confirmation*. Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) requires \"atomic per-product resets with stable identities, request-time edit cutoffs, hard publish gates.\" Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s *Reset Isolation Proof* mandates a success-and-fault-injection test that \"restores only the target baseline while preserving Domain Identity.\"\n\nThis ticket decides: the Reset Window mechanism; the per-domain atomic transactional scope (where the Source Identity Registry lives during reset — T3.2); the Reset Evidence schema; the Reset Confirmation flow; and the recovery path on partial failure.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline — T4.1 (Reset is the inverse).\n- Reset Isolation Proof test cases — those are T8.x acceptance gates.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-29","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-dz7","title":"Decide the Source Identity Registry survival-across-resets mechanism","description":"## Blocked by\n\n- #24 — T3.1 Decide the UUIDv7 implementation strategy\n\n---\n\n## Question\n\nHow does the Source Identity Registry durably associate a Source Identity with its Domain Identity across independent Product Resets, given that the Product Reset discards the domain's local rows but must preserve identity continuity?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Source Identity Registry* as \"the durable association between a Source Identity and the matching Domain Identity across independent resets.\" *Product Reset* \"discards that domain's local changes while preserving Domain Identities.\" So the registry must outlive the rows it tracks — either by living outside the reset transaction's scope, by being re-affirmed during import, or by being logically separate from the domain tables.\n\nDecision [#6](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/6) specifies \"preserve exact source identities, normalise values, restrict relationships and deletion.\" Decision [#7](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/7) specifies \"stable identities\" through the import/reset cycle.\n\nThis ticket decides: the registry's table location (per-domain vs a shared registry table), whether it lives inside or outside the reset transaction, and how it is (re)populated during Product Import.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Product Import pipeline shape — T4.1.\n- The Product Reset transactional strategy — T4.2.\n\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-25","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-en4","title":"Decide the Search Document shape and Search Surface rules per entity","description":"## Question\n\nFor each Domain Entity type across the three products, what fields form the Search Document, what is the Search Surface (allowed non-sensitive text/metadata), and what Search Tier (hybrid / lexical-only / excluded) does each entity receive?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md` defines *Search Document*, *Search Surface*, and *Search Tier*. Decision [#8](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/8) requires \"three search tiers, a pinned OpenAI embedding profile, RRF-ranked product-owned results, and transactional FTS with verified after-commit vectors.\" *Search Surface* explicitly excludes raw Blob Assets, contact data, location data, and relationship-only rows.\n\nThis ticket decides: per-entity Search Document field selection; the Surface allow-list per entity; Tier assignment per entity; and how the document retains product identity for Federated Search.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- FTS5/vec0 virtual table schema — T6.2.\n- The embedding pipeline mechanics — T6.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-31","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-jyk","title":"Decide the Team Artefact schema and Team Owner policy boundary","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the schema for Team Artefacts (Saved Searches, Team Dashboards), how is ownership attributed to a creator (per `CONTEXT.md`'s Team Artefact definition), and what is the Team Owner's authority boundary over them?\n\n## Context\n\n`CONTEXT.md`: *Team Artefact* is \"a team-visible Saved Search or Dashboard owned by one team and attributed to its creator, even after that creator's membership ends. It does not alter shared sample data or restrict a Sample Product.\" *Team Owner* \"has no authority over shared sample data.\" Decision [#9](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/9) separates \"team-owned artefacts from global capabilities.\"\n\nThe Livewire teams starter ships Team, Membership, TeamOwner concept. This ticket integrates Team Artefact as a new domain concept owned by Team but attributed to User.\n\nThis ticket decides: Team Artefact table shape (Saved Search + Dashboard); attribution + ownership semantics; the Team Owner's policy over them; and how a Saved Search integrates with the Federated Search result page (T6.4).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Team-readable URLs / slug policy — out of scope per map #1.\n- Portfolio Card layout — T7.1.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-36","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-m7d","title":"Define cross-product reporting and overall-panel read models","description":"## Question\n\nWhich cross-product comparisons and operational summaries belong in the overall Filament panel, and what explicit query/read-model boundaries should permit useful joins without pretending the three sample domains share business identities?","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-10","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-nn3","title":"Define the authorization, audit, and configurable-dashboard package boundary","description":"## Question\n\nWhich packages, if any, should provide authorization administration, audit presentation, and configurable dashboard forms while preserving the established global-capability, team-artifact, four-panel, and code-owned-card boundaries?\n\n## Starting evidence\n\n- The user approved Spatie Permission v8 as the authorization substrate and Filament Shield as a conditional role/permission administration and policy-generation layer, with panel-qualified permission names.\n- Spatie Activitylog v5 is the supplementary selected-event audit trail; durable Reset Evidence remains its dedicated domain record.\n- Lara Zeus Activity Timeline is only an optional presentation adapter.\n- Filament Shield Enhanced is deferred until a concrete non-CRUD permission requires it and its early-release risk is reassessed.\n- Guardian and Flex Fields are not selected: they either blur the team/data boundary or conflict with the code-owned, allow-listed portfolio-card catalogue.\n\nThe ticket will make the boundary implementation-ready without installing or scaffolding packages.","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-13","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-qaq","title":"Verify Filament 5 + Shield + Livewire teams starter co-installation","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the current, verified installation and configuration path for Filament 5 alongside the Livewire teams starter (Fortify 1.37 + Flux 2.13 + Livewire 4.1 + passkeys + 2FA), and does Filament Shield coexist with the starter's existing Team policy and Fortify's auth routes?\n\n## Context\n\nThe repo was bootstrapped from the Laravel Livewire starter kit, which ships with its own auth flow, team models, and policies (`app/Concerns/HasTeams.php`, `app/Policies/TeamPolicy.php`, `app/Http/Middleware/EnsureTeamMembership.php`, Fortify response contracts under `app/Http/Responses/`). Filament 5 ships its own panel auth. Decision [#2](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/2) requires Filament 5 + Livewire SFC co-existence but did not specify the install recipe.\n\nFilament 5 is recent; verify against the official Filament docs and any migration notes for Livewire 4 / Flux 2 / Fortify 1.37 compatibility. Capture conflicts with the teams starter's middleware and Fortify's response contracts.\n\nThis ticket resolves a fact-finding question by reading official Filament 5 docs, Livewire 4 docs, and the teams-starter source.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The decision on panel install order — T1.1 consumes this research.\n- Spatie Permission + Shield deeper integration — T3.3.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-19","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-r6j","title":"Verify Herd SQLite extension loading mechanics","description":"## Question\n\nHow does Laravel Herd (Pro/Dot/Custom) load native SQLite extensions at connection time, what configuration or environment variables are required, and is the `sqlite-vec` extension persistent across Herd's PHP-FPM worker restarts?\n\n## Context\n\nDecision [#12](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/12) requires the Native Extension Verification Matrix to prove \"Herd CLI/HTTP\" capability. Herd is the macOS arm64 target for the Two-Environment Operational Gate. Herd ships its own SQLite/PHP; the verified load mechanism may differ from a vanilla PHP setup.\n\nVerify against official Herd documentation and any community guidance on native SQLite extension loading under Herd. Capture the precise `php.ini` or per-connection PDO attribute mechanism (`PDO:: SQLITE_ATTR_LOAD_EXTENSION` or sqlite3 extension config).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Linux CI load mechanism (standard PHP PDO; covered by T2.1).\n- The Extension Connection Gate decision — T2.2.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-23","labels":["wayfinder:research"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-yfz","title":"Decide the git branch, PR, and dependency-pinning strategy","description":"## Question\n\nWhat is the git workflow for this implementation effort — trunk-based with short-lived feature branches vs long-lived per-stage branches — and what is the dependency-pinning policy (when do composer/pnpm locks get bumped, who reviews)?\n\n## Context\n\nThe repo currently runs trunk-based (recent merge was PR #14 → main). Decision [#11](https://github.com/s-a-c/samples-20260717/issues/11)'s Two-Environment Operational Gate makes every PR run Linux x86_64 CI; release candidates additionally require Herd evidence. Long-lived branches complicate the per-PR gate.\n\nThis ticket decides: branch strategy; PR granularity (per-ticket vs per-stage); merge method (squash vs merge vs rebase); dependency-bump cadence and assignee; and whether Renovate/Dependabot is enabled.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The CI pipeline itself — T8.3.\n- Specific dependency choices — each stage ticket owns its own.\n","status":"open","priority":0,"issue_type":"task","created_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","updated_at":"2026-07-19T23:35:46Z","external_ref":"gh-39","labels":["wayfinder:grilling"],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"samples-20260717-jl2.10","title":"SAGE substrate: fix node-operator key path (infra blocker)","description":"Infra fault outside this repo, blocking ticket jl2.3 (SAGE token mint). Container sage-substrate-sage-1 cannot issue new MCP tokens: HTTP 503 'node operator identity unavailable'. Root cause: SAGE_AGENT_KEY unset in container env -\u003e service reads /root/.sage (directory) as the key file -\u003e 'HTTP MCP: cannot load agent key — transport disabled'. FIX: add env SAGE_AGENT_KEY=/root/.sage/agent.key to the sage service in /Users/s-a-c/infra/sage-substrate/docker-compose.yml, then docker compose up -d --force-recreate sage. Verify: docker exec -e SAGE_AGENT_KEY=/root/.sage/agent.key sage-substrate-sage-1 sage-gui mcp-token create --name samples-20260717-probe returns a token, then revoke it. Affects every project needing new tokens (samples-20260717, chinook rotations). Owner: infra/sage-substrate.","status":"closed","priority":0,"issue_type":"bug","owner":"71233932+s-a-c@users.noreply.github.com","created_at":"2026-07-18T23:22:59Z","created_by":"s-a-c","updated_at":"2026-07-19T00:48:55Z","closed_at":"2026-07-19T00:48:55Z","close_reason":"Fixed: data/config.yaml had agent_key_file: '' (empty string overrode the env var, making the service read /root/.sage as a file). Set agent_key_file: /root/.sage/agent.key in /Users/s-a-c/infra/sage-substrate/data/config.yaml; also added SAGE_AGENT_KEY env to both sage + sage-mcp-proxy services in docker-compose.yml as defense-in-depth. Restarted sage container. Verified: log shows 'node operator key registered' (INFO); probe token minted + revoked; real samples-20260717 token minted (id 80e9e94d-e142-414e-8b0b-ff0f0820cd04). Note: infra compose + config.yaml changes are in the substrate repo, not committed here.","labels":["blocker","wayfinder:map","wayfinder:task"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"samples-20260717-jl2.10","depends_on_id":"samples-20260717-jl2","type":"parent-child","created_at":"2026-